WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.751
RMF involves many stakeholders.

00:00:01.751 --> 00:00:05.465
Effective risk management depends on collaboration between system owners,

00:00:05.465 --> 00:00:08.766
security professionals, engineers, architects, assessors, privacy personnel, executives,

00:00:08.766 --> 00:00:12.893
authorizing officials, operations teams, and sometimes mission or business owners.

00:00:12.893 --> 00:00:17.432
System owners are typically responsible for understanding the system, its purpose,

00:00:17.432 --> 00:00:20.733
its boundaries, its users, and its control responsibilities.

00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:24.860
They help coordinate the information needed for categorization, control selection,

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:26.098
implementation, and monitoring.

00:00:26.098 --> 00:00:30.225
Security and privacy professionals help interpret requirements, guide control selection,

00:00:30.225 --> 00:00:33.939
support assessment preparation, manage findings, and advise on risk.

00:00:33.939 --> 00:00:39.716
Engineers and architects turn many of those requirements into working technical and operational controls.

00:00:39.716 --> 00:00:42.192
Assessors evaluate control implementation and effectiveness.

00:00:42.192 --> 00:00:46.319
They provide findings that help the organization understand strengths, weaknesses,

00:00:46.319 --> 00:00:47.969
and areas needing remediation.

00:00:47.969 --> 00:00:53.334
Authorizing officials or accountable leaders make risk decisions based on the available evidence.

00:00:53.334 --> 00:00:57.873
Their role is important because risk acceptance is a leadership responsibility,

00:00:57.873 --> 00:00:59.937
not only a technical one.

00:00:59.937 --> 00:01:04.063
Clear responsibilities prevent RMF from becoming a confusing paperwork exercise.

00:01:04.063 --> 00:01:08.603
When everyone knows what they own, evidence is easier to collect,

00:01:08.603 --> 00:01:13.142
controls are easier to maintain, and risk decisions are better informed.

00:01:13.142 --> 00:01:16.856
For individual employees, the key takeaway is shared responsibility.

00:01:16.856 --> 00:01:22.633
You may not own the entire RMF process, but your work may support controls,

00:01:22.633 --> 00:01:25.109
evidence, monitoring, remediation, or operational decisions.

00:01:25.109 --> 00:01:27.585
Clear role definition also reduces friction.

00:01:27.585 --> 00:01:32.125
People can respond faster when they know who owns a control,

00:01:32.125 --> 00:01:35.426
who approves a change, who tracks a finding,

00:01:35.426 --> 00:01:39.140
and who needs to be informed when risk changes.
